 ## Design decisions
 
-- Cache keys leave the tenant out on purpose: each tenant is pinned to its own worker pool with its own Cache instance (BILL-1874), so it would be redundant.
+- Cache keys always include the tenant id. They used to leave it out on the assumption that each tenant has its own Cache instance (BILL-1874), but a Cache can be shared across tenants in prod, which leaked results between tenants (BILL-2291).
diff --git a/src/cache.py b/src/cache.py
index 5205441..727b738 100644
--- a/src/cache.py
+++ b/src/cache.py
@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@ class Cache:
         return len(self._store)
 
 
-def make_key(op, entity_id):
-    # Build the cache key from the operation name and the entity id
-    return f"{op}:{entity_id}"
+def make_key(tenant_id, op, entity_id):
+    # The tenant must be part of the key: one Cache instance can serve several
+    # tenants, and entity ids (periods, customer ids) collide across tenants.
+    return f"{tenant_id}:{op}:{entity_id}"
